From 0396f4f8028cf5409e8314e63c562126acb5d62e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: steam-machine-hdmi21 <hdmi21@localhost>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 18:52:37 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] drm/amd/display: re-verify the HDMI FRL link capability on
 every hotplug

With amdgpu.dcfeaturemask=0x402 an FRL-capable HDMI sink trains at FRL on
the first detect after boot (an LG C4 comes up at 4K144, 10-bit 4:4:4), but
every later detect ends TMDS-only: a TV re-handshake after wake, a TV input
switch, or a write of 0 then 1 to the connector's debugfs trigger_hotplug
all leave the link at 600 MHz TMDS until the machine is rebooted.

The detect path recreates the sink as SIGNAL_TYPE_HDMI_TYPE_A, so only a
destructive verify can promote it back to FRL;
verify_link_capability_non_destructive() cannot. Which one runs is decided
by should_verify_link_capability_destructively(), whose HDMI FRL branch
sets destrictive = true and then gives it away again:

	} else if (link->dc->config.skip_frl_pretraining) {
		for (i = 0; i < MAX_PIPES; i++) {
			if (pipes[i].stream != NULL &&
				pipes[i].stream->link == link) {
				/*If link is already active, skip PHY programming*/
				if (link->link_status.link_active) {
					destrictive = false;
				}
			}
		}
	}

dc->config.skip_frl_pretraining is set unconditionally in every DCN 3.x
resource file, dcn32_resource.c and dcn321_resource.c included, so it is
always true on this Navi 33. gamescope keeps the CRTC lit across a hotplug,
so link_status.link_active is always true too once the desktop is up. The
result is that every detect after the first one is non-destructive and the
link can never be promoted to FRL again.

Mainline no longer has that global gate. The same branch there reads

	} else if (link->local_sink->edid_caps.panel_patch.skip_frl_pre_training) {

i.e. a per-panel EDID quirk that is off unless a specific panel asks for
it, so a current kernel retrains FRL destructively on every hotplug. See
commit c953b39f9487 ("drm/amd/display: Reintroduce "Force validation link
training on all ASICs"").

7.2.4-valve1 has no panel_patch.skip_frl_pre_training field to switch to,
so drop the else-if and its pipe loop entirely, leaving is_hdmi_frl_in_use()
as the only reason to go non-destructive. That is mainline behaviour for
every panel without the quirk. The pipe walk's two locals go with it as
they have no other user.

Trade-off: a hotplug now tears the link down and retrains it, so the output
blanks for a moment where it previously stayed lit at the wrong rate. That
is what mainline does anyway, and it is the price of coming back at 4K144
instead of 4K120 4:2:0.
---
 .../gpu/drm/amd/display/dc/link/link_detection.c   | 14 --------------
 1 file changed, 14 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/dc/link/link_detection.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/dc/link/link_detection.c
index 75dcc39126bc..1d9c6b3dfd34 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/dc/link/link_detection.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/dc/link/link_detection.c
@@ -926,23 +926,9 @@ static bool should_verify_link_capability_destructively(struct dc_link *link,
 	} else if (dc_is_hdmi_signal(link->local_sink->sink_signal) && link->link_enc &&
 				link->link_enc->features.flags.bits.IS_HDMI_FRL_CAPABLE &&
 				link->local_sink->edid_caps.max_frl_rate != 0) {
-		int i = 0;
-		struct pipe_ctx *pipes =
-				link->dc->current_state->res_ctx.pipe_ctx;
-
 		destrictive = true;
 		if (is_hdmi_frl_in_use(link)) {
 			destrictive = false;
-		} else if (link->dc->config.skip_frl_pretraining) {
-			for (i = 0; i < MAX_PIPES; i++) {
-				if (pipes[i].stream != NULL &&
-					pipes[i].stream->link == link) {
-					/*If link is already active, skip PHY programming*/
-					if (link->link_status.link_active) {
-						destrictive = false;
-					}
-				}
-			}
 		}
 	}
 
